= Shortcut learning =

== Overview ==
'''Shortcut learning''' is a research framing for cases in which a learning system relies on comparatively easy predictive cues or decision rules that perform well on the training or standard benchmark distribution but fail to capture the intended robust task strategy.

The term became prominent in deep-learning analysis through Geirhos and colleagues.

== Shortcuts ==
Possible shortcuts include:

* image backgrounds;
* watermarks;
* acquisition-device signatures;
* text style or lexical cues;
* dataset-specific artifacts;
* demographic/site proxies;
* answer-format regularities.

A cue is a shortcut relative to the intended task and evaluation context.

== Generalization failure ==
Shortcut rules can achieve excellent in-distribution accuracy.

They become visible when conditions change, confounding cues are removed, counterfactual examples are introduced, or evaluation targets a more robust notion of the task.

== Spurious correlations ==
Shortcuts often exploit [[Spurious correlation|spurious correlations]].

However:

<pre>
spurious correlation = relation/data property
shortcut learning = learner's use of an easy decision rule
</pre>

The concepts overlap but are not identical.

== Scope ==
Shortcut learning is a useful explanatory framework, not a theorem that explains every failure of deep learning.

Models can use both robust and shortcut features simultaneously.

Whether a feature is an undesirable shortcut depends partly on task intent and deployment conditions.

== Related concepts ==
[[Spurious correlation]], [[Distribution shift]], [[Generalization (machine learning)]], [[Overfitting]], and [[Inductive bias]] provide the generalization context.

== References ==
* Geirhos et al., “Shortcut Learning in Deep Neural Networks”, ''Nature Machine Intelligence'' 2 (2020), 665–673.
* Schramowski et al., “Making Deep Neural Networks Right for the Right Scientific Reasons”, ''Nature Machine Intelligence'' 2 (2020), 476–486.
* recent PMLR literature on shortcut mitigation and spurious features.

